<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_a5da5097_9fb3_42c2_a6b2_b104f06ff773">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_66281f4c_ce3b_46e0_bd24_b5cdcce93094"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_b655d54f_d812_4635_a1a5_8949ee4383fc"/>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Glass</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An amorphous ceramic material obtained through a glass transition.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">To be considered a true glass, an amorphous ceramic must have been formed via the glass transition (usually by cooling a liquid fast enough to freeze its chaotic structure in place). A glass lacks long-range translational order. Its structure is disordered and isotropic (the same in all directions).</rdfs:comment>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">Borosilicate</core:example>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Glass</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">To be considered a true glass, an amorphous ceramic must have been formed via the glass transition (usually by cooling a liquid fast enough to freeze its chaotic structure in place). A glass lacks long-range translational order. Its structure is disordered and isotropic (the same in all directions).</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">An amorphous ceramic material obtained through a glass transition.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A glass is not always a mechanical solid. From a strict physics and thermodynamics standpoint, glass is a state of matter. Specifically, an amorphous network trapped in a structurally frozen state. Because this state is entirely dependent on temperature, glass can exist as a flowing, viscous liquid just as easily as it can exist as a brittle windowpane.
The boundary between "liquid glass" and "solid glass" isn't a sharp melting point like you see when ice turns to water. Instead, it is a gradual, temperature-dependent zone called the glass transition temperature Tg.</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_8b0923ab_b500_477b_9ce9_8b3a3e4dc4f2">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.2777/417118"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780521405980"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_bc37743c_37c4_4ec7_9d58_d1aae5567352"/>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuum_mechanics"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ContinuumSubstance</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A substance that is made of sufficiently large number of smaller parts such that it is the bearer of qualities that can exist only by the fact that it is a sum of parts.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Continuum is usually an assumption made relative to a specific entity and scale of observation. We can define a continuum substance as a substance for which continuum mechanics (i.e., solid or fluid mechanics) can be used as a model for the description and prediction of its behaviour.
Paramount to this definition is the applicability of the continuum assumption, that is usually made by comparing the scale of the constituents' phenomena (e.g., mean free path of a molecule in a gas) and the scale of the macro-entity (e.g., the container of the gas).</rdfs:comment>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">A single continuum individual can be the whole fluid in a pipe.</core:example>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ContinuumSubstance</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">Continuum is usually an assumption made relative to a specific entity and scale of observation. We can define a continuum substance as a substance for which continuum mechanics (i.e., solid or fluid mechanics) can be used as a model for the description and prediction of its behaviour.
Paramount to this definition is the applicability of the continuum assumption, that is usually made by comparing the scale of the constituents' phenomena (e.g., mean free path of a molecule in a gas) and the scale of the macro-entity (e.g., the container of the gas).</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A substance that is made of sufficiently large number of smaller parts such that it is the bearer of qualities that can exist only by the fact that it is a sum of parts.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A continuum is the bearer of properties that are generated by the interactions of its fundamental parts, such as viscosity and thermal or electrical conductivity. In fact, viscosity or electrical conductivity are properties of the macro-entity (e.g., solid, fluid), and not of the constituents (e.g., atoms or molecules).</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_3c5b33a1_4f28_41f9_b725_1994df79bf55">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/computerscience#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_f84b1b92_1dc8_4146_99f0_b03cd53e455b"/>
