<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_660a4964_0333_4663_bc66_e93ef59b0679">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.2777/417118"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_bc37743c_37c4_4ec7_9d58_d1aae5567352"/>
  <owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="#EMMO_8b0923ab_b500_477b_9ce9_8b3a3e4dc4f2"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">MesoscopicSubstance</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A matter that is not a particulate matter and that does not fullfill the criteria of being a continuum substance.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Mesoscopic substances typically exist on a length scale between atoms and molecules and macroscopic (bulk) substances. They are typically in the range of some nanometers to about a micrometer and often exhibit unique physical properties due to their size and shape. Due to their nature of  they show no clear homonegenity in properties, and cannot be qualified as solid or liquid, but still have some criteria (e.g., structural, shape) that can be used to define them.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">carbon nanotube</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">nanoparticle</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">quantum dot</core:example>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">MesoscopicSubstance</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">Mesoscopic substances typically exist on a length scale between atoms and molecules and macroscopic (bulk) substances. They are typically in the range of some nanometers to about a micrometer and often exhibit unique physical properties due to their size and shape. Due to their nature of  they show no clear homonegenity in properties, and cannot be qualified as solid or liquid, but still have some criteria (e.g., structural, shape) that can be used to define them.</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A matter that is not a particulate matter and that does not fullfill the criteria of being a continuum substance.</elucidation>
</owl:Class>

