<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_4e9fd152_147b_4564_8999_089608f41aff">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.12743"/>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_b9695e87_8261_412e_83cd_a86459426a28"/>
  <iupacReference rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.12743"/>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amorphous_solid"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">AmorphousState</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Amorphous substances are characterized by the absence of regular long-range order within their atomic networks, distinguishing them from highly ordered crystalline lattices. This structural disorder prevents the formation of regular macroscopic geometric facets. Thermodynamically and physically, this manifests as a lack of a distinct melting point, irregular fracture behavior, diffuse X-ray diffraction patterns, and isotropic mechanical, optical, and electrical properties.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">State of matter characterised by the absence of long-range molecular order.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:altLabel xml:lang="en">AmorphousNetwork</core:altLabel>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">AmorphousState</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">Amorphous substances are characterized by the absence of regular long-range order within their atomic networks, distinguishing them from highly ordered crystalline lattices. This structural disorder prevents the formation of regular macroscopic geometric facets. Thermodynamically and physically, this manifests as a lack of a distinct melting point, irregular fracture behavior, diffuse X-ray diffraction patterns, and isotropic mechanical, optical, and electrical properties.</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">State of matter characterised by the absence of long-range molecular order.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">If you look at the fundamental IUPAC (International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry) definition, "amorphous" simply means a material lacks long-range translational order. Under this pure structural definition, liquids, gases, plasmas, and supercritical fluids are all amorphous states of matter. They have no crystalline lattice, no unit cells, and no repeating 3D patterns. If you take an X-ray diffraction scan of a liquid, you get the exact same broad, featureless "amorphous halo" that you get from a piece of solid glass. Therefore, structural physicists routinely refer to liquids as amorphous networks.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">In the absence of stress, the structure and properties of the amorphous state are generally isotropic at a large-enough scale, as in liquids, but they can readily develop functional/property anisotropy under the right conditions.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">One of the most common technique to define if a substance is lacking of a regular structure (amorphous) is to look at its x-ray diffraction pattern. A diffuse and poorly defined pattern is typical of an amorphous structure, while a regular reproducible pattern is typical of a crystalline structure.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">Some degree of local order about 1 nm  in length may exist in the amorphous state.</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_b02aaadc_592d_4d65_bf11_eee06bd9f80d">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_16f2fe60_2db7_43ca_8fee_5b3e416bfe87"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_dae32a4a_d8da_4047_81b0_36a9713fdce1"/>
