<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_4207e895_8b83_4318_996a_72cfb32acd94">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780080966656"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780471736967"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_220b7201_d277_4dca_bf6a_5a5e2c4062dd"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_6e9cb807_fc68_4bcf_b3ba_5fccc887c644"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Material</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A material is condensed matter categorized according to some criteria based on kind, quality or quantity. An intended use is typically associated with each of the classes used to categorize a material entity.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">It is quite hard to define what a material is, given the wide range of use of such term, especially in order to distinguish it from the more general matter concept. First, we decided to restrict the term's domain with respect to matter, by making a material always an ordinary and condensed matter entity, thus excluding antimatter, particulate matter (e.g., molecular entities such as atoms and molecules), and mesoscopic entities (e.g., nanoparticles). Then, to call material every entity that can be categorised according to the typical criteria used in material science (e.g., by structure, function, size, isotropy). Such criteria usually categorise a substance according to kind, quality or quantity and are expressed in order to facilitate the identification of specific class of substances according to an intended use. In short, a material is what is called material in material science.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Material</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">It is quite hard to define what a material is, given the wide range of use of such term, especially in order to distinguish it from the more general matter concept. First, we decided to restrict the term's domain with respect to matter, by making a material always an ordinary and condensed matter entity, thus excluding antimatter, particulate matter (e.g., molecular entities such as atoms and molecules), and mesoscopic entities (e.g., nanoparticles). Then, to call material every entity that can be categorised according to the typical criteria used in material science (e.g., by structure, function, size, isotropy). Such criteria usually categorise a substance according to kind, quality or quantity and are expressed in order to facilitate the identification of specific class of substances according to an intended use. In short, a material is what is called material in material science.</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A material is condensed matter categorized according to some criteria based on kind, quality or quantity. An intended use is typically associated with each of the classes used to categorize a material entity.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A instance of a material (e.g. nitrogen) can represent any state of matter. The fact that the individual also belongs to other classes (e.g. liquid) would reveal the actual form in which the material is found.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">A material may be either a fluid or a solid substance. Gas is not a material, because the material concept is here restricted to classical material science, focusing exclusively on condensed matter.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">Materials are here restricted to condensed matter. The core paradigm of materials science is the Structure-Property-Processing-Performance relationship. This paradigm relies entirely on atoms being close enough to interact continuously, thus excluding non-condensed matter from the materials field:
- Structure: refers to crystal lattices, defects, grain boundaries, or amorphous molecular networks. Gases, by definition, lack any long-range or short-range structural arrangement because their particles are in constant, random motion.
- Properties: materials science measures collective mechanical properties (elastic modulus, hardness), thermal properties (conductivity), and electrical properties (band gaps). A gas cannot support a shear stress, deform plastically, or possess a structural microstructure.
Because gases lack a fixed structure, they are traditionally handed over to chemistry, thermodynamics, or fluid mechanics, rather than materials science.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">The concept of material is not an ontological one i.e., does not originate from the entity per se, but originates from actual materials science practitioners and their categorisation habits. For this reason, all materials subclasses are grouped according to classification criteria (e.g., by compactness, by function, by size).</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_0b0dc439_fe4b_4e59_a5f6_655b0bf48559">
