<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_c5e9e390_8836_4440_b4fd_c906fdd4f438">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780471736967"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_4db521a3_f80f_436b_b9c7_e4b52b1383ea"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CeramicMaterial</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds (oxides, carbides, nitrides) that are typically hard, strong, brittle, corrosion-resistant, and good insulators.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Ceramic is defined commonly as an inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds. Ceramics are mostly oxides, nitrides and carbides.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:altLabel xml:lang="en">Ceramic</core:altLabel>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">Al₂O₃ based compunds</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">Concrete</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">Glass</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">SiC or Si₃N₄ based compunds</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">ZrO₂ based compunds</core:example>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CeramicMaterial</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">Ceramic is defined commonly as an inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds. Ceramics are mostly oxides, nitrides and carbides.</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">An inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds (oxides, carbides, nitrides) that are typically hard, strong, brittle, corrosion-resistant, and good insulators.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A ceramic with a disordered, random structure frozen in place, is considered amorphous. An amorphous ceramic can be categorised according to its formation process, depending on the fact that it went or not through a glass transition in glass or amorphous solid.
The distinction is about the thermodynamic state of the material. Because a true glass was once a liquid, it occupies a specific metastable energy state dictated by how fast it was cooled. If you take an amorphous solid thin film (e.g., made by PVD) and heat it up, it will often crystallize abruptly or behave unpredictably because it has a highly stressed, ultra-high-energy structure. A true glass, when heated, will predictably soften into a supercooled liquid once it hits its Tg.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">While the word "ceramic" instantly brings to mind brittle, rigid objects like coffee mugs, brick, or technical alumina plates, a ceramic is defined purely by its chemical bonds and atomic structure, as an inorganic non-metallic substance.
Here we embrace this definition in its wide and literal meaning, even if in practical cases the term ceramic is restricted only to high-temperature, corrosion and wear resistant materials.</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_d4d9fa8b_5998_4398_8aa4_05bdafa83e11">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_d8a7aac9_984a_4254_ab3b_7d8b1c061149"/>
