<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/statesofmatter#"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780471400653"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780521408448"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_4e9fd152_147b_4564_8999_089608f41aff"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_87ac88ff_8379_4f5a_8c7b_424a8fff1ee8"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_fc9222ed_14af_419b_b9df_b6a8a04e9d38"/>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gas"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GaseousState</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gas is a state of matter in which particles (e.g., atoms or molecules) have enough kinetic energy to overcome the intermolecular forces binding them together, so they move freely and independently, filling whatever container holds them. Unlike a liquid, a gas has no definite volume and is readily compressible.
A gaseous state can be categorised univocally whether the substance sits below or above critical temperature T𞁞​, because that alone determines whether a liquid–vapor phase boundary exists. Below T𞁞​ the gaseous phase is condensable by isothermal compression; at and above T𞁞 no amount of pressure produces a distinct liquid.
A gas differs from a plasma in that its particles remain electrically neutral and un-ionized; once a significant fraction of particles ionize, the system becomes a plasma, which responds to electromagnetic fields collectively.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gaseous state is a compressible fluid, a state of matter that has no fixed shape and no fixed volume.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:altLabel xml:lang="en">Gas</core:altLabel>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GaseousState</core:prefLabel>
  <OWLRL2DLAxioms xml:lang="en">Disjoint Union Of: SupercriticalGaseousState, Vapor</OWLRL2DLAxioms>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">A gas is a state of matter in which particles (e.g., atoms or molecules) have enough kinetic energy to overcome the intermolecular forces binding them together, so they move freely and independently, filling whatever container holds them. Unlike a liquid, a gas has no definite volume and is readily compressible.
A gaseous state can be categorised univocally whether the substance sits below or above critical temperature T𞁞​, because that alone determines whether a liquid–vapor phase boundary exists. Below T𞁞​ the gaseous phase is condensable by isothermal compression; at and above T𞁞 no amount of pressure produces a distinct liquid.
A gas differs from a plasma in that its particles remain electrically neutral and un-ionized; once a significant fraction of particles ionize, the system becomes a plasma, which responds to electromagnetic fields collectively.</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A gaseous state is a compressible fluid, a state of matter that has no fixed shape and no fixed volume.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">"Vapor" and "gas" are often used interchangeably in casual speech, but in the strict sense they are different branches of the tree, divided by T𞁞. A vapor can be liquefied by pressure alone; a gas cannot. This is why "water vapor" is correct (water's Tc​ = 647 K is far above ambient) while room-temperature N₂ or O₂ is properly a gas.</note>
</owl:Class>

