<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_80fe82d4_f4c1_43a1_98dc_ee5fc7927e19">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_12b2ec1e_fb89_468a_a51d_97c2a6db297c"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PH</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">At about 25 °C aqueous solutions with:
pH &lt; 7 are acidic;
pH = 7 are neutral;
pH &gt; 7 are alkaline.
At temperatures far from 25 °C the pH of a neutral solution differs significantly from 7.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Number quantifying the acidic or the alkaline character of a solution, equal to the negative of the decimal logarithm of ion activity aH+ of the hydrogen cation H+
pH = −10 log(a_H+).</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Written as pH</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PH</core:prefLabel>
  <IEVReference xml:lang="en">https://www.electropedia.org/iev/iev.nsf/display?openform&amp;ievref=114-01-21</IEVReference>
  <ISO80000Reference xml:lang="en">For more details, see ISO 80000-9:2009, Annex C</ISO80000Reference>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">Number quantifying the acidic or the alkaline character of a solution, equal to the negative of the decimal logarithm of ion activity aH+ of the hydrogen cation H+
pH = −10 log(a_H+).</elucidation>
  <iupacReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.P04524</iupacReference>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_bc37743c_37c4_4ec7_9d58_d1aae5567352">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_41cecfc2_bb6b_45f3_b6cc_e7740d931afe"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_57d977ab_0036_4779_b59a_e47620afdb9c"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Substance</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A matter entity characterised by the fact that it possesses some homogeneous properties or identification criteria.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A substance is an aggregate of particulate matter that shows an homogeneity of property, according to some criteria. A substance is an intermediate concept between generic matter and more specific categorizations. A substance can be either continuum or mesoscopic.
The word substance here refers to a concept that is unrelated with aristotelic philosophy.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">A litre of water.</core:example>
  <core:example xml:lang="en">A pen can be classified as a substance (although it has parts with different properties) since it can be identified as being solid.</core:example>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Substance</core:prefLabel>
  <OWLRL2DLAxioms rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Disjoint Union Of: MesoscopicSubstance, ContinuumSubstance</OWLRL2DLAxioms>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">A substance is an aggregate of particulate matter that shows an homogeneity of property, according to some criteria. A substance is an intermediate concept between generic matter and more specific categorizations. A substance can be either continuum or mesoscopic.
The word substance here refers to a concept that is unrelated with aristotelic philosophy.</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A matter entity characterised by the fact that it possesses some homogeneous properties or identification criteria.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A substance is always 4D self-connected, meaning that it represents only entities within a time interval that ensure self-connectivity. For example, the entity made of a bag of powder that is parceled up into portions and used in different applications is a single substance.</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_fd6559e8_ef94_460c_9dfc_bad5c68d63b4">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/reference/standardmodel-full#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_fa3c9d4d_9fc9_4e8a_82c1_28c84e34133a"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">GaugeBoson</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A bosonic elementary particle that mediates interactions among elementary fermions, and thus acts as a force carrier.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">GaugeBoson</core:prefLabel>
  <OWLRL2DLAxioms rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DisjointUnionOf: WeakBoson, Photon, Gluon</OWLRL2DLAxioms>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A bosonic elementary particle that mediates interactions among elementary fermions, and thus acts as a force carrier.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">All known gauge bosons have a spin of 1 and are hence also vector bosons.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">Gauge bosons can carry any of the four fundamental interactions of nature.</note>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauge_boson</wikipediaReference>
</owl:Class>

