  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ElectricCurrentPhasor</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A representation of current as a sinusoidal integral quantity using a complex quantity whose argument is equal to the initial phase and whose modulus is equal to the root-mean-square value.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ElectricCurrentPhasor</core:prefLabel>
  <ISO80000Reference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">6-49</ISO80000Reference>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A representation of current as a sinusoidal integral quantity using a complex quantity whose argument is equal to the initial phase and whose modulus is equal to the root-mean-square value.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A phasor is a constant complex number, usually expressed in exponential form, representing the complex amplitude (magnitude and phase) of a sinusoidal function of time. Phasors are used by electrical engineers to simplify computations involving sinusoids, where they can often reduce a differential equation problem to an algebraic one.</note>
  <qudtReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/ElectricCurrentPhasor</qudtReference>
  <wikidataReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q78514596</wikidataReference>
</owl:Class>

