<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_f1025834_0cd2_42a1_bfeb_13bec41c8655">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://dictionary.iucr.org/Crystal"/>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_220b7201_d277_4dca_bf6a_5a5e2c4062dd"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_f00fb163_48c9_4c59_b6c2_4205c082aa54"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CrystallineMaterial</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A substance is in a crystal material if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern. The word essentially means that most of the intensity of the diffraction is concentrated in relatively sharp Bragg peaks, besides the always present diffuse scattering.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CrystallineMaterial</core:prefLabel>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A substance is in a crystal material if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern. The word essentially means that most of the intensity of the diffraction is concentrated in relatively sharp Bragg peaks, besides the always present diffuse scattering.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A crystalline material is always condensed matter since it cannot be gaseous. In fact, in a gas we observe:
a) Thermal Dominance: The kinetic energy kB T of the particles completely overwhelms any attractive intermolecular forces
b) Mean Free Path: Gas particles are separated by vast distances relative to their size and are in constant, chaotic, random motion. They collide and scatter continuously.
Because a gas has absolutely zero correlation between the position and orientation of neighboring particles over time, it possesses no translational or orientational order which is the definition of a crystalline state.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">Crystalline material can be solid or liquid. In fact, crystallinity is defined by atomic or molecular order, not by mechanical rigidity. Historically, "crystal" and "solid" were used interchangeably because the most common materials with long-range atomic order happen to be rigid.
However, the modern definition focuses entirely on symmetry and diffraction patterns. If a material has a regular, repeating spatial arrangement that produces a sharp, discrete X-ray diffraction pattern, it possesses crystallinity. If a material can permanently resist a shear force without flowing, it is mechanically a solid. Because these two properties are governed by different physical mechanisms, they can be decoupled.</note>
  <note xml:lang="en">Crystalline material does not imply that the entity is a single crystal, but only that its parts are in crystalline state (single crystal). Thus, a poly crystalline entity, made of crystal grains packed together is 99% a crystalline material, even if they are not oriented in the same way.</note>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_2b43c6ff_4a2d_4eb1_9797_e279c52894d3">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_00ed0f82_f20e_4749_9780_380f43398466"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">NonFerrousAlloy</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A metal alloy in which iron is not the primary constituent.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">NonFerrousAlloy</core:prefLabel>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A metal alloy in which iron is not the primary constituent.</elucidation>
</owl:Class>

<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_ffbacbc4_c3be_4cc5_9539_94344e72f7a6">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/isq#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="#EMMO_19d925d0_2cf1_40e5_a391_1a99d68409c9"/>
