<owl:Class rdf:about="#EMMO_76cfad65_2147_44d6_86da_dc89c27c1782">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
  <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="urn:isbn:9780471736967"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="#EMMO_f1025834_0cd2_42a1_bfeb_13bec41c8655"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PolyCrystallineMaterial</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A material composed of a collection of many small individual crystalline units called grains.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From a strict thermodynamic and physical standpoint, a polycrystalline material is in a crystalline state. The word "state" often implies a single, uniform condition, and a polycrystal is full of messy, mismatched grain boundaries. However, in materials science and condensed matter physics, the "state" of a material is defined by its atomic structure and thermodynamic phase, not by its macro-scale uniformity.
Because the vast majority of the matter possesses long-range translational order, the bulk material exhibits the thermodynamic properties of the crystalline state (such as a sharp, distinct melting point, rather than the gradual softening seen in amorphous states like glass).</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PolyCrystallineMaterial</core:prefLabel>
  <conceptualisation xml:lang="en">From a strict thermodynamic and physical standpoint, a polycrystalline material is in a crystalline state. The word "state" often implies a single, uniform condition, and a polycrystal is full of messy, mismatched grain boundaries. However, in materials science and condensed matter physics, the "state" of a material is defined by its atomic structure and thermodynamic phase, not by its macro-scale uniformity.
Because the vast majority of the matter possesses long-range translational order, the bulk material exhibits the thermodynamic properties of the crystalline state (such as a sharp, distinct melting point, rather than the gradual softening seen in amorphous states like glass).</conceptualisation>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">A material composed of a collection of many small individual crystalline units called grains.</elucidation>
  <note xml:lang="en">A polyscrystalline state is a mosaic of tiny single crystals. Inside any single grain, the atoms are perfectly ordered. However, each grain is tilted or rotated at a different angle relative to its neighbors. Where these mismatching orientations meet, they form irregular atomic interfaces known as grain boundaries. Most engineered metals, alloys, and ceramics are polycrystalline.
Inside a polycrystal, more than 99% of the atoms sit precisely on a periodic, highly ordered crystalline lattice. They have undergone a distinct phase transition (solidification/crystallization) into a crystalline state. The only atoms not in a perfect lattice are the thin, few-atom-wide walls at the grain boundaries.</note>
</owl:Class>

