<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#ElectronVolt">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#EMMO_6795a4b8_ffd0_4588_a581_a9413fe49cac"/>
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#EMMO_f6070071_d054_4b17_9d2d_f446f7147d0f"/>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/siacceptedspecialunits-abox#"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ElectronVolt</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An electron volt (eV) is the energy that an electron gains when it travels through a potential of one volt. You can imagine that the electron starts at the negative plate of a parallel plate capacitor and accelerates to the positive plate, which is at one volt higher potential. Numerically 1 eV approximates 1.6x10⁻¹⁹ joules, where 1 joule is 6.2x10¹⁸ eV. For example, it would take 6.2x10²⁰ eV/sec to light a 100 watt light bulb.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The amount of energy gained (or lost) by the charge of a single electron moving across an electric potential difference of one volt.</rdfs:comment>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ElectronVolt</core:prefLabel>
  <sche:unitCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A53</sche:unitCode>
  <EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">1.602177e-19</EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf>
  <EMMO_daf7a9b7_18ef_4e73_9fb9_75adfd9800e8 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">eV</EMMO_daf7a9b7_18ef_4e73_9fb9_75adfd9800e8>
  <dbpediaReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://dbpedia.org/page/Electronvolt</dbpediaReference>
  <definition xml:lang="en">The amount of energy gained (or lost) by the charge of a single electron moving across an electric potential difference of one volt.</definition>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">An electron volt (eV) is the energy that an electron gains when it travels through a potential of one volt. You can imagine that the electron starts at the negative plate of a parallel plate capacitor and accelerates to the positive plate, which is at one volt higher potential. Numerically 1 eV approximates 1.6x10⁻¹⁹ joules, where 1 joule is 6.2x10¹⁸ eV. For example, it would take 6.2x10²⁰ eV/sec to light a 100 watt light bulb.

-- QUDT</elucidation>
  <iupacReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.E02014</iupacReference>
  <qudtReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/EV</qudtReference>
  <ucumCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">eV</ucumCode>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electron_volt?oldid=344021738</wikipediaReference>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

