<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#InverseSquarePlanckMass">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#EMMO_15344998_4490_4b40_b242_c3d92e5cdc27"/>
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#EMMO_6aa04359_50d6_43d7_b3a7_296bd391bf7d"/>
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#EMMO_b081b346_7279_46ef_9a3d_2c088fcd79f4"/>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/otherunits-abox#"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">InverseSquarePlanckMass</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In physics, the Planck mass, denoted by m_P, is the unit of mass in the system of natural units known as Planck units. It is defined so that ≈  1.2209 × 10 GeV/c₀ = 2.17651(13) × 10 kg, (or 21.7651 μ g), where c₀ is the speed of light in a vacuum, G is the gravitational constant, and ℏ is the reduced Planck constant. Particle physicists and cosmologists often use the reduced Planck mass, which is ≈ 4.341 × 10 kg = 2.435 × 10 GeV/c. The added factor of 1/√(8π) simplifies a number of equations in general relativity. Quantum effects are typified by the magnitude of Planck's constant.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
  <core:altLabel xml:lang="en">Inverse Square Planck Mass</core:altLabel>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">InverseSquarePlanckMass</core:prefLabel>
  <EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">2111089000000000.0</EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf>
  <EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">0.0</EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">In physics, the Planck mass, denoted by m_P, is the unit of mass in the system of natural units known as Planck units. It is defined so that ≈  1.2209 × 10 GeV/c₀ = 2.17651(13) × 10 kg, (or 21.7651 μ g), where c₀ is the speed of light in a vacuum, G is the gravitational constant, and ℏ is the reduced Planck constant. Particle physicists and cosmologists often use the reduced Planck mass, which is ≈ 4.341 × 10 kg = 2.435 × 10 GeV/c. The added factor of 1/√(8π) simplifies a number of equations in general relativity. Quantum effects are typified by the magnitude of Planck's constant.

-- QUDT</elucidation>
  <qudtReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/PER-PlanckMass2</qudtReference>
  <unitSymbol rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">/mₚ²</unitSymbol>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_units</wikipediaReference>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

