  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Dyne</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to 10 micronewton. Equivalently, the dyne is defined as 'the force required to accelerate a mass of one gram at a rate of one centimetre per square second'. The dyne per centimetre is the unit traditionally used to measure surface tension.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Dyne</core:prefLabel>
  <EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">1e-05</EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf>
  <EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">0.0</EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd>
  <elucidation xml:lang="en">In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to In physics, the dyne is a unit of force specified in the centimetre-gram-second (CGS) system of units. One dyne is equal to 10 micronewton. Equivalently, the dyne is defined as 'the force required to accelerate a mass of one gram at a rate of one centimetre per square second'. The dyne per centimetre is the unit traditionally used to measure surface tension.

-- QUDT</elucidation>
  <qudtReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/DYN</qudtReference>
  <ucumCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">dyn</ucumCode>
  <unitSymbol rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">dyn</unitSymbol>
  <wikipediaReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyne?oldid=494703827</wikipediaReference>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

